\section{Symbolically Check a Concrete Edge Shuffle}

The checker needs an edge's original copy map and the set of
locations live through it. Give each location a distinct symbolic atom
at entry; equal numeric test values might hide an incorrect swap.
Assume locations are independent, all inputs and the scratch location
can be named, and the target shuffle has been decoded to ordinary
location-to-location moves. There are no implicit writes or side
effects in this model. The checker compares the shuffle's effect on
every promised output, not on scratch locations that are dead afterward.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Copy map $C$, moves $M$, live-through locations $L$
\Let{O}{\operatorname{distinctAtoms}(\operatorname{locations}())}
\Let{S}{\operatorname{copy}(O)}
\Let{E}{\operatorname{snapshot}(O,L)}
\ForAll{$(d,s)\in C$}
  \To{Parallel requirements read the entry snapshot.}
  \Let{E[d]}{O[s]}
\EndFor
\ForAll{$(d,s)\in M$ in emitted order}
  \To{Moves read the state left by earlier moves.}
  \Let{S[d]}{S[s]}
\EndFor
\State \Return $S$ agrees with $E$ on its entire domain
\end{sudocode}

For the assignments $a\gets b$, $b\gets a$, and $c\gets a$,
let entry hold $(a,b,c,d)=(8,3,1,17)$ and let $d$ be live through.
The expected tuple is $(3,8,8,17)$. Copying $a$ into a fresh scratch,
then $b$ into $a$, then scratch into $b$ and $c$, produces that tuple.
Using $d$ as the scratch also produces the right first three values
but leaves $d=8$; the checker rejects. A test that examines only
merge destinations would accept it.

The symbolic state after each move identifies the original source
of each location's current value. Distinct atoms prove equality for
all assignments of entry values in this restricted language, not
just for the example numbers. Checking costs one update per move
and one comparison per promised location, besides setup. Overlapping
subregisters require a byte or bit representation: independent atoms
for a register and its low part would miss a real interaction.
Placement is also a separate obligation. A correct sequence executed
on the wrong successor edge still changes the program.

\section{Validate Before Removing the Evidence}

Interpret the parallel assignment over distinct symbolic input tokens, and interpret the emitted move sequence over the same input state. Compare every destination and every location live through the edge. This catches the swap error as well as damage to unrelated state; a separate CFG check ensures the moves execute only on their intended edge. The dossier's Chapter~37, \emph{Compiler as a Constraint Solver}, offers a complementary view of allocation and move selection, but a cost-minimal move sequence still needs the same semantic check. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-210} looks at validation across a larger pass boundary.
